From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:05:24 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: Add the Google TG4C panel

Add a driver for the Google Pixel 9a's TG4C command-mode AMOLED panel.
The bootloader powers the panel, initialises it and leaves it running
at 60 Hz; the driver takes it over as it is, as the S6E3HC4 driver does,
and never resets it or touches its supplies:

 - sleep in and out around a power cycle of the display pipeline,
   keeping the panel's registers;
 - 60 and 120 Hz through drm_panel_set_refresh_rate() (0x2f 0x02 and
   0x2f 0x00, Google's tg4c_change_frequency());
 - the brightness as a DCS backlight over Google's normal range, levels
   1 to 3628 (up to 1200 nits), with Google's default of 1829. The
   levels above 3628 are high brightness mode and are left for a later
   control of their own.

Command sequences, modes and sizes from Google's MIT-licensed panel
driver.

Tested on the Pixel 9a (7.3.0-rc6-omamobile-tegu-s2mpg15-m1, this file
at blob 19c89b162a5e, which used 4095 as maximum and default) with
gs-dsim-cmd and gs-decon: the desktop runs at 120 Hz, DPMS off puts the
panel to sleep and DPMS on wakes it, and the brightness follows the
backlight across its range. This version changes only the brightness
range and default and the physical size, and drops an unused flag.

Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/google-modules/display/panels/tegu/+/e3a801fb038fdd1bbaec36d387f27bd99f10757a/panel-gs-tg4c.c
Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
